1
00:00:02,262 --> 00:00:04,171
With all of that, we learned a lot about

2
00:00:04,203 --> 00:00:07,316
Deno and I wanna conclude this section about

3
00:00:07,316 --> 00:00:10,540
the core APIs in a second and move on to the

4
00:00:10,540 --> 00:00:12,738
standard library, which is the next logical

5
00:00:12,770 --> 00:00:13,107
step.

6
00:00:13,653 --> 00:00:16,428
Not before talking about the Deno program

7
00:00:16,541 --> 00:00:19,445
lifecycle though, because that is important.

8
00:00:20,231 --> 00:00:22,990
Your Deno program, for example, the one from

9
00:00:23,039 --> 00:00:25,333
the assignment, has a lifecycle.

10
00:00:25,864 --> 00:00:27,430
And that simply means that there are

11
00:00:27,494 --> 00:00:30,593
specific events which are automatically

12
00:00:30,868 --> 00:00:32,724
emitted and to which you can listen

13
00:00:33,354 --> 00:00:36,304
when certain things happen in your script

14
00:00:36,337 --> 00:00:36,952
execution.

15
00:00:39,006 --> 00:00:40,572
If we run our demo file,

16
00:00:41,347 --> 00:00:43,123
script execution of course starts and

17
00:00:43,140 --> 00:00:45,449
executes the file start to end, but during

18
00:00:45,481 --> 00:00:48,471
that execution, we got these various events,

19
00:00:48,536 --> 00:00:49,833
these various steps.

20
00:00:50,416 --> 00:00:53,025
For example, we get the load event right

21
00:00:53,074 --> 00:00:55,018
when the file was compiled and loaded and

22
00:00:55,018 --> 00:00:55,456
the execution started.

23
00:00:57,128 --> 00:00:59,515
And we can listen to that event and execute

24
00:00:59,515 --> 00:01:01,005
code in response to it.

25
00:01:01,742 --> 00:01:03,921
We can also execute asynchronous code there

26
00:01:03,921 --> 00:01:04,578
if we want to.

27
00:01:05,187 --> 00:01:08,311
So this allows us to execute some extra code

28
00:01:08,311 --> 00:01:10,138
which we don't wanna put into our main

29
00:01:10,666 --> 00:01:13,983
script code whenever the script starts.

30
00:01:14,800 --> 00:01:16,642
Well, then we have the regular script

31
00:01:16,642 --> 00:01:18,661
execution of all the code we have in the

32
00:01:18,661 --> 00:01:21,529
script, and then we also have the unload

33
00:01:21,529 --> 00:01:24,365
event that's going to trigger right before

34
00:01:24,429 --> 00:01:25,999
the script will shut down.

35
00:01:26,673 --> 00:01:29,736
The script will end whenever the end of the

36
00:01:29,864 --> 00:01:31,885
file is reached, so whenever there is no

37
00:01:31,885 --> 00:01:34,579
more code to execute, and if there are no

38
00:01:34,675 --> 00:01:37,546
ongoing processes. For example, later you

39
00:01:37,578 --> 00:01:40,464
will see when we start a web server, the

40
00:01:40,464 --> 00:01:42,774
script is never going to end because it's

41
00:01:42,774 --> 00:01:44,682
continuously waiting for new incoming

42
00:01:44,778 --> 00:01:45,307
requests.

43
00:01:45,917 --> 00:01:48,643
But whenever your process does end, for

44
00:01:48,643 --> 00:01:51,129
example, also with the web server, if that

45
00:01:51,129 --> 00:01:53,534
server crashes the unload event is

46
00:01:53,614 --> 00:01:56,179
triggered. And there you also can listen to

47
00:01:56,179 --> 00:01:58,102
it and execute code, though

48
00:01:58,647 --> 00:01:59,994
only synchronous code.

49
00:02:00,523 --> 00:02:03,456
So no code that uses promises or callbacks

50
00:02:03,456 --> 00:02:06,021
to do something asynchronous. You can't send

51
00:02:06,021 --> 00:02:08,522
an HTTP request there, for example, or at

52
00:02:08,522 --> 00:02:10,766
least you won't be able to react to the

53
00:02:10,766 --> 00:02:13,459
response. You can only do synchronous things

54
00:02:13,491 --> 00:02:14,501
here if you want to.

55
00:02:15,559 --> 00:02:17,867
And these events can be useful to have some

56
00:02:17,964 --> 00:02:20,112
general setup work that should be done

57
00:02:20,224 --> 00:02:22,821
whenever the script starts and to have some

58
00:02:22,933 --> 00:02:25,339
general cleanup work whenever the script

59
00:02:25,435 --> 00:02:25,724
ends.

60
00:02:26,301 --> 00:02:28,307
Now let's quickly see how we can listen to

61
00:02:28,307 --> 00:02:28,772
these events.

62
00:02:29,767 --> 00:02:31,404
For that, I'll add another new file,

63
00:02:32,174 --> 00:02:34,629
demo_lifeCycle.ts,

64
00:02:35,447 --> 00:02:36,940
and that will be a very trivial file.

65
00:02:38,175 --> 00:02:39,539
For one, I'll console.log

66
00:02:40,117 --> 00:02:43,069
main script, and that's my main script

67
00:02:43,069 --> 00:02:45,428
execution code, so the main code that should

68
00:02:45,492 --> 00:02:46,294
be in my script.

69
00:02:46,936 --> 00:02:49,584
But I also wanna listen to that load event,

70
00:02:49,888 --> 00:02:52,119
and for listening, we got 2 options.

71
00:02:52,875 --> 00:02:56,079
We can reach out to this window object and

72
00:02:56,095 --> 00:02:58,640
set the onload property

73
00:02:59,300 --> 00:03:01,763
equal to a function, for example, equal to

74
00:03:01,780 --> 00:03:02,663
an arrow function.

75
00:03:03,563 --> 00:03:05,475
This function will then run

76
00:03:06,069 --> 00:03:07,965
whenever the onload event occurs,

77
00:03:08,961 --> 00:03:11,483
and we'll get an event object here. And then

78
00:03:11,516 --> 00:03:12,748
I can console.log

79
00:03:14,678 --> 00:03:16,088
onload event

80
00:03:16,705 --> 00:03:18,958
and also console.log the event object.

81
00:03:21,137 --> 00:03:23,720
The alternative way of listening would be

82
00:03:23,864 --> 00:03:24,297
that you

83
00:03:25,613 --> 00:03:28,629
add an event listener to window and listen

84
00:03:28,661 --> 00:03:31,244
to the load event. So not onload here, but

85
00:03:31,389 --> 00:03:32,014
just load.

86
00:03:32,608 --> 00:03:34,260
And then the second argument here is the

87
00:03:34,292 --> 00:03:36,458
function that should be executed upon the

88
00:03:36,458 --> 00:03:36,747
event.

89
00:03:37,421 --> 00:03:40,710
And there I will add that same code, onload

90
00:03:40,710 --> 00:03:42,635
event via listener.

91
00:03:43,582 --> 00:03:46,085
The important difference just like in the

92
00:03:46,150 --> 00:03:46,519
browser

93
00:03:47,129 --> 00:03:49,104
where you also have different ways of adding

94
00:03:49,136 --> 00:03:49,746
event listeners,

95
00:03:50,324 --> 00:03:53,038
is that with that you can only define one

96
00:03:53,150 --> 00:03:53,600
function.

97
00:03:54,419 --> 00:03:54,772
If I

98
00:03:55,494 --> 00:03:57,919
set a new function to onload, it will

99
00:03:58,112 --> 00:04:00,135
override the previously added function.

100
00:04:00,938 --> 00:04:03,073
On the other hand, you can add multiple

101
00:04:03,170 --> 00:04:05,658
event listeners with addEventListener. So if

102
00:04:05,658 --> 00:04:07,874
you need multiple listeners which should all

103
00:04:08,147 --> 00:04:11,021
run, you should use addEventListener If you

104
00:04:11,053 --> 00:04:13,205
have just one code snippet that needs to

105
00:04:13,205 --> 00:04:15,679
execute, you can use the onload property.

106
00:04:17,076 --> 00:04:19,597
Now with that, we're printing onload twice

107
00:04:19,726 --> 00:04:21,814
in the end because we set up a listener with

108
00:04:21,814 --> 00:04:24,062
this approach and with addEventListener.

109
00:04:25,186 --> 00:04:27,113
And thereafter, I'm going to copy this.

110
00:04:28,157 --> 00:04:29,313
We can do the same for

111
00:04:30,390 --> 00:04:30,968
onload

112
00:04:32,397 --> 00:04:35,689
and print onload event. And here also we

113
00:04:35,689 --> 00:04:37,167
want to listen to unload

114
00:04:37,716 --> 00:04:39,953
and print onUnload here as well.

115
00:04:42,076 --> 00:04:43,470
Now let's see how that works.

116
00:04:43,996 --> 00:04:45,097
If I now save this

117
00:04:45,939 --> 00:04:49,632
and I run this demo lifecycle.ts file,

118
00:04:51,460 --> 00:04:53,417
you see script execution finished. And what

119
00:04:53,417 --> 00:04:56,475
you see is we got the main script actually

120
00:04:56,507 --> 00:04:57,996
being output first.

121
00:04:58,675 --> 00:05:00,422
Now the order in which we see this here

122
00:05:00,520 --> 00:05:02,955
shouldn't confuse you, shouldn't matter too

123
00:05:02,955 --> 00:05:05,066
much. These listeners, the onLoad listeners,

124
00:05:05,099 --> 00:05:06,317
do also run right at the beginning just

125
00:05:06,317 --> 00:05:09,223
because the event needs to be processed

126
00:05:09,276 --> 00:05:09,629
succeeded.

127
00:05:10,255 --> 00:05:11,202
This main script,

128
00:05:11,861 --> 00:05:14,157
the simple console.log statement was running

129
00:05:14,253 --> 00:05:16,180
first and logging this to the console, but

130
00:05:16,260 --> 00:05:18,139
onload also runs right when the script

131
00:05:18,139 --> 00:05:18,588
starts.

132
00:05:19,648 --> 00:05:21,174
And you see the event object which we're

133
00:05:21,254 --> 00:05:23,389
getting is a very simple one, it just has

134
00:05:23,421 --> 00:05:24,883
the isTrusted flag in there.

135
00:05:26,071 --> 00:05:29,073
And then we have the onload event once the

136
00:05:29,122 --> 00:05:30,535
script execution finishes.

137
00:05:31,369 --> 00:05:34,003
And to see a difference, I will now actually

138
00:05:35,528 --> 00:05:38,355
wait for some user input by going to demo

139
00:05:38,499 --> 00:05:38,933
input

140
00:05:39,945 --> 00:05:42,867
and copying this code we had in there

141
00:05:42,932 --> 00:05:43,333
before.

142
00:05:44,361 --> 00:05:46,898
And below my main script console.log, I will

143
00:05:47,589 --> 00:05:50,769
again listen to that input here, get my

144
00:05:50,801 --> 00:05:51,202
input.

145
00:05:52,069 --> 00:05:54,093
By the way, close all our files. If you

146
00:05:54,093 --> 00:05:55,907
should be getting an error regarding

147
00:05:57,144 --> 00:05:58,750
constant names which you're using in

148
00:05:58,814 --> 00:06:00,709
multiple files, that is fine to do.

149
00:06:01,239 --> 00:06:03,455
My IDE just doesn't understand that these

150
00:06:03,568 --> 00:06:06,013
files don't run as one big process,

151
00:06:06,770 --> 00:06:08,607
which is why it does complain about name

152
00:06:08,639 --> 00:06:10,105
clashes. This code is fine.

153
00:06:10,637 --> 00:06:13,133
So here we now got the code to listen to

154
00:06:13,133 --> 00:06:13,877
user input,

155
00:06:14,443 --> 00:06:15,626
and now you'll see a difference.

156
00:06:16,289 --> 00:06:18,216
If I now run this code

157
00:06:18,977 --> 00:06:19,706
again here,

158
00:06:21,320 --> 00:06:24,046
deno run deno lifecycle.ts,

159
00:06:25,450 --> 00:06:25,967
you see

160
00:06:27,645 --> 00:06:28,613
we have main script,

161
00:06:29,420 --> 00:06:31,100
please enter your message. Then we got the

162
00:06:31,181 --> 00:06:33,346
onload events, of course, because the script

163
00:06:33,623 --> 00:06:34,193
just loaded.

164
00:06:34,762 --> 00:06:37,022
But we don't see the onUnload events yet

165
00:06:37,151 --> 00:06:38,818
because the script is still running.

166
00:06:40,036 --> 00:06:42,473
And if I cancel the script, for example,

167
00:06:42,521 --> 00:06:45,438
with Ctrl+C, we also don't see it because I

168
00:06:45,487 --> 00:06:47,987
hard canceled it. If it would have crashed

169
00:06:47,987 --> 00:06:49,879
because of an error, on the other hand, we

170
00:06:49,975 --> 00:06:50,440
would see it.

171
00:06:51,354 --> 00:06:53,710
Now if I do enter something here,

172
00:06:54,961 --> 00:06:57,445
you see now it finishes as planned and we

173
00:06:57,445 --> 00:07:00,219
see the onUnload event being emitted as

174
00:07:00,315 --> 00:07:00,427
well.

175
00:07:01,455 --> 00:07:03,833
So that's how the program lifecycle works.

176
00:07:04,090 --> 00:07:06,050
You will not need this all the time, but it

177
00:07:06,098 --> 00:07:08,685
is important to be aware of these 2 events,

178
00:07:09,006 --> 00:07:12,236
load and unload, so that you can do setup

179
00:07:12,364 --> 00:07:14,517
and cleanup work if you need to.
